- [Your guide to stopping chargeback fraud](https://www.riskified.com/learning/chargebacks/chargeback-fraud/): Chargeback fraud is commonly referred to as first-party fraud. You may also see it described as friendly fraud, liar buyer, or first-party misuse.
- [What is “chargeback insurance?”](https://www.riskified.com/learning/chargeback-protection/chargeback-insurance/): "Chargeback insurance” is, first and foremost, a myth. 
- [What is a chargeback guarantee?](https://www.riskified.com/learning/chargeback-protection/chargeback-guarantee/): A chargeback guarantee is a contractual obligation made by a fraud protection provider to a merchant. It guarantees that the provider will reimburse the merchant for any approved card-not-present orders that turn out to be fraudulent, and thus results in a reversal, or “chargeback,” from a bank. 
- [A complete guide to streamlining chargeback dispute management](https://www.riskified.com/learning/chargebacks/chargeback-dispute/): A chargeback dispute is a process through which a merchant can combat illegitimate chargeback claims.
- [What is payment fraud?](https://www.riskified.com/learning/ecommerce-fraud/payment-fraud/): Payment fraud is, at its core, a false or illegitimate transaction. Before the Internet, payment fraud typically consisted of a simple case of bounced checks or erroneous chargebacks. However, with the advent of ecommerce, it’s become more complex.
- [9 types of ecommerce fraud: Examples, trends, and prevention strategies](https://www.riskified.com/learning/ecommerce-fraud/): Ecommerce fraud is the use of stolen payment information, compromised accounts, or deceptive tactics to make unauthorized purchases or exploit online store policies. It most commonly occurs in card-not-present transactions and includes fraud schemes such as account takeover, chargeback fraud, abuse of merhant policies including refund and return fraud. For merchants, ecommerce fraud results in chargebacks, lost revenue, and operational costs.

